SCOPE: internal/models/major_expense.go, internal/services/majorexpenses/engine.go,
internal/services/dataloader/major_expenses.go, internal/handlers/whatif/sync.go,
web/templates/components/whatif/sync-preview.html — all SY1 territory per SY-RUN-SPEC.md.
Foreign HC-territory dirty files (internal/handlers/dashboard/**, internal/models/dashboard.go,
internal/services/metrics/**, internal/services/mcpsvc/spend/**,
internal/services/dataloader/transfers_test.go, web/templates/components/kpis.html) were not
touched and are attributed to the concurrent HC run per the coordinator's live update; not
probed further (a coordinator message mid-task confirmed internal/handlers/dashboard doesn't
currently compile tree-wide because of HC's own calibration work — irrelevant to SY1, whose
four packages don't import dashboard handlers; internal/handlers/whatif built and tested clean
throughout).

ATTACK 1 — Hash-collision on duplicate transactions (twins). D-SY-b/AC2 return
map[string]models.MajorExpense keyed by transaction Hash; sync.go's aggregation loop looks up
`exclusions[t.Hash]` per row. If two DISTINCT transactions (different StableID) shared an
identical Hash and diverged in classification (one pinned into a flagged group, its twin
naturally matching a different unflagged def), the Hash-keyed map would misclassify both.
VERIFIED FALSE ALARM: internal/services/dataloader/loader.go:717 `deduplicateTransactions`
runs unconditionally inside LoadDataContext (loader.go:366), keyed on exact Hash, keeping only
the first occurrence — before computeDashboardSync ever sees the data. Two rows with identical
Hash cannot coexist in the TransactionSet computeDashboardSync operates on. Confirmed by
reading assignStableIDs' own comment ("two rows with the same content Hash are exact
duplicates, and dedup keeps the first") and the call graph LoadData -> LoadDataContext ->
deduplicateTransactions. This attack is dead; no FAIL from it.

ATTACK 2 — syncPlanHash determinism across preview/apply. Read plan.ExcludedGroups
construction: aggregated into a local `map[string]excludedTotal` keyed by def.ID, then
converted to a slice and `sort.Slice`'d by Name before assignment — matches the existing
TestComputeDashboardSync_ExcludedGroupsOrderIsDeterministic (two live computeDashboardSync
calls, hash-compared). No map field survives into syncPlan (syncExcludedGroup has no map/slice
members needing further sorting), so json.Marshal's encoding is deterministic. No defect found.

ATTACK 3 — D-SY-e HI+flagged overlap, first-def-wins trap, pin precedence: read the sync.go
loop (HI check strictly before the exclusions-map lookup, both `continue`), and exercised the
package's own TestComputeDashboardSync_ExcludesFlaggedMajorExpenseGroup fixture logic by
inspection — order and one-time exclusion match D-SY-e as written. No defect found.

ATTACK 4 — REFUND NETTING IN THE DISPLAYED GROUP TOTAL (CONFIRMED DEFECT). sync.go's
aggregation loop for excluded groups does:
    agg.Total += math.Abs(t.Amount)
i.e. it sums the ABSOLUTE VALUE of every row assigned to a flagged group, regardless of sign.
AC5 explicitly requires a refund (positive-amount, Outflow-typed) inside a flagged group to be
"skipped like its siblings" — the worker's classifier-level test proves exclusion-membership
for such a row, but no test (classifier-level or sync.go-level) exercises what a refund does to
the group's DISPLAYED Total. This directly contradicts the established, already-shipped
convention for the exact same concept (a major-expense group total) one package over:
internal/handlers/majorexpenses/handlers.go:~366-370 computes group totals as
`total += -t.Amount` specifically "so refunds reduce the group total instead of inflating it
via AbsAmount" (identical language repeated at ~409 for the unmatched-total sibling
computation). SY1's sync.go reintroduces the exact bug that sibling code was written to avoid.

PROOF (throwaway probe, added/run/deleted inside internal/handlers/whatif/, confirmed removed
via `git status --porcelain` before writing this verdict):
  Fixture: 4x "Car Loan Payment,-500,Auto Payment" (four months) + one
  "Car Loan Payment,500,Auto Payment" (a same-description credit/reversal — classified Outflow
  because "loan payment" hits classifier.NeverIncomeKeywords, and stays positive per
  ClassifyTransactions' "keep credits positive" rule; all five rows exact-amount-match the
  car-loan def's ExpectedMin=ExpectedMax=500 flagged def).
  True net excluded from consideration: 4*500 - 500 = $1500.
  Worker's code produced: plan.ExcludedGroups[0].Total = $2500.00, Count=5.
  ($2500 = 5 * abs(500) — every row summed as a positive magnitude, refund included.)
  Test output:
    group Total = 2500.00 (net-would-be=1500.00 gross-would-be=2500.00) Count=5
    DEFECT: excluded group Total = 2500.00, want net 1500.00
  A $1000 swing (a $500 credit inflates the shown total by $1000 instead of reducing it by
  $500) on a figure the preview renders directly to the user via formatNumber
  ("{{Name}} — ${{formatNumber .MonthlyAmount}}/mo ({{.Count}} transactions)", D-SY-d/f) — this
  is exactly the "rendered figures must be true" defect class (ruling 2026-08-29b lineage): the
  displayed exclusion total does not equal what was actually netted out of the plan.
  NewMonthlyExpenses itself is unaffected (the row is still unconditionally `continue`d out of
  totalExpenses regardless of sign) — the bug is confined to the informational ExcludedGroups
  Total/MonthlyAmount, but that is precisely the field D-SY-d exists to make trustworthy
  ("preview transparency").
  Neither the worker's tests nor `.swarm/tier3/SY1/accept.sh` (grepped, not executed per
  instructions) contain "refund" or "reversal" — this scenario was untested at both the primary
  and oracle layers.

Fix would be a one-line change (`agg.Total += -t.Amount` guarded by the same sign convention
the sibling handlers.go code uses, or equivalently sum signed and take abs once) — small,
squarely in SY1's own sync.go file, not a scope-exceeding rewrite.

STRONGEST ATTACK THAT FAILED TO BREAK IT: Attack 1 (Hash-collision on twins) — a real-looking
risk given the map is Hash-keyed and this exact codebase has prior art for StableID-vs-Hash
identity subtlety (transaction_pins.go, duplicate_decisions.go), but falsified by the
unconditional exact-Hash dedup that runs before computeDashboardSync ever sees the data.

VERDICT: FAIL on Attack 4 (refund/credit rows in a flagged group inflate the displayed
ExcludedGroups.Total instead of netting, contradicting AC5's refund requirement's evident
intent and the codebase's own established convention for the identical concept).
